//BUG="a levelled break out of `while true` leaves a path that falls off the end"
//ERR any

// 33 with the `brk` one loop further in. The level is what makes it escape: a
// bare `brk` here would leave only the `for`, and the `while true` would still
// never fall through — that is
// 05_functions/32_a_break_from_an_inner_loop_does_not_escape_a_while_true.cpl,
// which has to keep compiling. The `2` reaches past the `for` and out of the
// `while true`, so `f(0)` leaves the loop and runs off the end of the body.
//
// So the check cannot just scan the loop's body for the keyword; it has to
// resolve each `brk` to the loop it actually targets. This is the case that
// separates the two.

fn f n
    while true
        for i 0..10
            if i > n
                brk 2
        ret 1

fn main
    out "{f(0)}"
